NUM-39: take a bio-retention underdrain without storage layer from the soil

In LIDSolver::biocellFluxRates(), a unit with no storage layer (a bio-retention cell
with a 0 in STORAGE layer or none, or a rain garden with a DRAIN line)
computed an underdrain rate from the head in the soil but subtracted it from
no layer, since f[STOR] is 0 and the soil only loses its percolation, which
was capped at the exfiltration rate. The drain then created water for as
long as the soil was above field capacity. Treat the zero-thickness storage
layer as full, as getStorageDrainRate() already does: soil percolation goes
to exfiltration first and the rest to the drain, up to the drain rate. With
no drain the result is unchanged.

Apply from the root of a Stormwater-Management-Model checkout at cb3e192b:
    git apply NUM-39_swmm600.patch

diff --git a/src/engine/hydrology/LID.cpp b/src/engine/hydrology/LID.cpp
index 9aa0c73..a39b7c1 100644
--- a/src/engine/hydrology/LID.cpp
+++ b/src/engine/hydrology/LID.cpp
@@ -859,9 +859,10 @@ public:
         }
         if (storageThickness == 0.0) {
             StorageEvap = 0.0;
-            maxRate = std::min(SoilPerc, StorageExfil);
+            maxRate = std::min(SoilPerc, StorageExfil + StorageDrain);
             SoilPerc = maxRate;
-            StorageExfil = maxRate;
+            StorageExfil = std::min(StorageExfil, maxRate);
+            StorageDrain = maxRate - StorageExfil;
             maxRate = (soilPorosity - soilTheta) * soilThickness / Tstep +
                       SoilPerc + SoilEvap;
             SurfaceInfil = std::min(SurfaceInfil, maxRate);
